# Product UX ≠ implementation chrome

Engineering patterns (SWR/React Query revalidation, polling, retries, optimistic updates) must not invent user-visible UI unless product explicitly owns that state.

## Doctrine

1. **User milestone ≠ backend job.** Show places and outcomes the learner understands (score saved, Week 1 ready, next task). Hide transport: syncing, revalidating, refetching, “calling the API”.
2. **Async is a status inside an owned place,** not a floating system ticker. Prefer one durable surface (e.g. Dashboard banner / task zone) over global “Syncing…” rows.
3. **Never mount/unmount chrome that shifts layout** for background work. If a status must exist, reserve space or put it on an already-owned card — better: delete it.
4. **One story per wait.** If the product already explains the wait (“Building your Week 1 plan”), do not add a second line that rephrases the network.

## Canonical miss (do not repeat)

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — SWR isValidating leaked as product UI; polls → layout jump / “system log” feel
{isValidating ? <div>Syncing latest progress</div> : null}

// ✅ GOOD — silent revalidate; product copy only where the journey owns the wait
{coach?.status === "pending" ? (
  <div>Building your Week 1 plan…</div>
) : null}
```

## Before shipping UI

Ask: *Would a learner understand this without knowing we use SWR/Supabase?* If no, it is implementation chrome — remove it or bury it in diagnostics / dev-only.

## When in doubt

Prefer invisible background refresh + honest product states (`pending` / `ready` / `error` + Retry) over any “sync / loading / updating” chrome at page chrome level.
